## Hypothesis
Bridge IX proved `lerp` is *a* constant-speed geodesic of `(Filtration α,
eInterleavingDist)`.  The adversarial hypotheses for Bridge X: (1) the geodesic is
*canonical* — the persistence functor `pullback` transports it exactly, and it is
closed under affine reparametrisation (self-similar); (2) the geodesic is
*Lipschitz-stable in its endpoints* — same-parameter interpolants of two geodesics
are convexly bounded, and `ε`-close endpoints give `ε`-close geodesics uniformly in
the parameter.

## Result
All confirmed, sorry-free.  `pullback_lerp` and `lerp_lerp` are `ring` identities on
weights (naturality and self-similarity), exhibiting `lerp` as a functorial,
straight-segment construction.  `eInterleavingDist_lerp_endpoints_le` is the
coordinatewise triangle inequality `(1−t)(F₁−F₂)+t(G₁−G₂)` pushed through
`ENNReal.ofReal` and the `⨆` of Bridge VIII's isometry; its corollary
`eInterleavingDist_lerp_endpoints_le_of_le` collapses the convex bound to `ε` because
`ofReal (1−t) + ofReal t = 1`.

## Insight
Every structural property of the geodesic is a *linearity phenomenon read through the
isometry*.  Once Bridge VIII identifies the metric with `⨆ σ, ofReal |weight gap|`,
naturality is "image-reindexing commutes with affine combination", self-similarity is
"affine combination of affine combinations is affine", and convex stability is "the
sup of triangle inequalities is a triangle inequality".  The functor, the
reparametrisation group, and the perturbation all act *coordinatewise* on weights, so
the supremum that defines the metric never sees them as anything but scalars.

## Failure analysis
The convex bound is genuinely *joint*: the naive route through the metric triangle
inequality `d(lerp F₁ G₁ t, lerp F₂ G₂ t) ≤ d(lerp F₁ G₁ t, lerp F₁ G₂ t) + …` loses
the `(1−t), t` weighting and only yields the `2ε`/additive bound; the sharp `ε` bound
*requires* the coordinatewise argument that keeps the two coefficients attached to
their respective endpoint gaps.  Uniqueness of the geodesic still fails (the `⨆`
leaves slack in non-maximising simplices free to wander — see FUTURE_DIRECTIONS,
Direction 1), so `lerp` remains one canonical geodesic among a convex family.
-/

end BoltzmannBridge